From 28b340be3f3b51ba7bc77b4bf5919026f0db2cf8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Broyojo <dha@xoba.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 18:48:34 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Clear a heap's owner when it is released

heap_release() put a thread's heap on the queue for reuse with its
owner_thread left set. A free decides between the local free list,
without atomics, and the atomic deferred lists by comparing owner_thread
with the current thread's id, and on Windows that id is the TEB address,
which Wine reuses for later threads. A new thread given the old thread's
TEB, but another heap from the queue, then took the released heap's
blocks for its own: it freed them onto the page's local free list and
changed the heap's page lists without atomics, while the thread that
took the heap from the queue allocated from the same pages. The thread
that released the heap did the same with the frees it made afterwards.
In a test with threads that start, free each other's blocks and exit,
most frees were of this kind, and with a short delay added where such a
free starts, the free lists were corrupted at once.

Clear owner_thread on release, so that until a thread takes the heap,
frees of its blocks go through the deferred lists, as frees from any
other thread do.
---
 rpmalloc/rpmalloc.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/rpmalloc/rpmalloc.c b/rpmalloc/rpmalloc.c
index a697ae9..afc91b7 100644
--- a/rpmalloc/rpmalloc.c
+++ b/rpmalloc/rpmalloc.c
@@ -1507,6 +1507,11 @@ heap_allocate(int first_class) {
 
 static inline void
 heap_release(heap_t* heap) {
+	// A released heap has no owner until a thread takes it from the queue. Keeping the old owner's
+	// id would let a later thread with the same id, which can be another thread entirely (on Windows
+	// the id is the TEB address, and TEBs are reused), free blocks into the heap as its own, without
+	// the atomic deferred lists, while the thread that took the heap allocates from it.
+	heap->owner_thread = 0;
 	heap_lock_acquire();
 	if (heap->prev)
 		heap->prev->next = heap->next;
